00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.280
Pretty much trying to. Set himself apart,&nbsp;
I guess, from progressives and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.009
Democrats who haven't focused on it as much.
Speaker 1: Okay. Yeah, I'll I'll I'll concede.&nbsp;

00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:05.220
Speaker 2: That.
Speaker 1: But I'll concede that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.380
at least for the purposes of your question.
Speaker 2: Okay. But.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.020
From my perspective, at least from the midterms,&nbsp;
it seems to me that the Democrats like Fetterman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:25.440
who didn't focus as much on crime, that they&nbsp;
didn't lose their elections, but the Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.080
in New York who kind of fearmonger over it,&nbsp;
I feel like they kind of campaigned against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.200
themselves in a way by kind of giving in to, like&nbsp;
the Republican narrative on crime themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.260
That's interesting. There's anything to that.
Speaker 1: I think they're I need to look a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:49.460
little more at the numbers and the specific races&nbsp;
you're talking about. But I do think that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:57.240
is a risk to the focusing on crime aspect to a&nbsp;
campaign, which is if the people you're talking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.720
don't actually believe that there is a&nbsp;
crime problem wherever it is that they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:11.340
it can absolutely backfire. And one of the things&nbsp;
that we have determined is that if you zoom out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.340
the violent crime rate in the United States has&nbsp;
been declining for a very long time. Yes, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.620
were some spikes in some places over the last&nbsp;
three years at different times. But the narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.080
of the dangerous blue states and blue cities is&nbsp;
certainly not true. At the state level. You see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:34.080
higher rates of crime in red states, even within&nbsp;
cities. For all the talk about New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:41.760
the crime rate and violent crime rate in Oklahoma&nbsp;
City is higher. And so I think the risk which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.820
you're astutely pointing out, is if it doesn't&nbsp;
resonate with people, if people are sort of like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:53.580
yeah, you know, I feel very safe and they're not&nbsp;
talking about anything else, I'm not going to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:58.740
for them. That is a real backfire that can happen.
Speaker 2: It's another thing for me, too, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.900
even if people don't feel safe where they are,&nbsp;
because they're, you know, listening to 24, seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:09.900
media that's worried about crime. Who are voters&nbsp;
going to go for? Are they going to go for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:16.320
Democrat that is doing tough on crime lite? Right.&nbsp;
Or are they going to go for the Republican that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.840
tough on crime, you know, ultra or something?
Speaker 1: Right. So you're saying either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.320
it's better for whoever is going&nbsp;
hardcore on the crime stuff.&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.049
Speaker 2: Well, I don't think&nbsp;
it's good for Democrats at all.&nbsp;

00:03:30.049 --> 00:03:31.680
Speaker 1: It's not good for Democrats either way.
Speaker 2: Effective.&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.120
Speaker 1: I think that you're on to something. I&nbsp;
think that you're on to something. I think we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.800
